\label{lem:central-core}
There is a connected bridgeless subgraph $H$ containing every crossing edge, with $Q\cap H$ a subpath of length $1\le h\le\rho$. It has a strong orientation whose only edge from $Y$ to $X$ is $Q_{c+1}\to Q_c$. Define $\varphi(v)=a(v)$ for $v\in X$ and $\varphi(v)=d+\rho-b(v)$ for $v\in Y$. This orientation satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{eq:core-distance}
\operatorname{dist}_{\mathpalette{\overarrow@\arrowfill@\relbar\relbar\rightharpoonup}{H}}(u,v)\le\varphi(v)-\varphi(u)+d+2h\qquad(u,v\in V(H)).
\end{equation}
Every component of $G-V(H)$ and all its attachments lie in one part.

\label{lem:axis-choice}
If \(t\ge1\), the centre \(p\), its farthest vertex \(q\), and the shortest path \(Q\) can be chosen so that a noncritical crossing edge has a value of \(m\) at most \(r+\varepsilon\). Consequently, \(\operatorname{dist}_{\mathpalette{\overarrow@\arrowfill@\relbar\relbar\rightharpoonup}{H}}(Q_c,Q_{c+1})\le2h+r-1+\varepsilon\).
If \(d\) is odd and \(t=0\), either the same choice with a value of \(m\) at most \(r+1\) is possible, or some edge has covering radius at most \(r\).

\label{lem:auxiliary-attachments}
Let $V$ be a component of $G-V(H)$, and let $S$ be its neighbours in $H$. Retain all edges within $V$ and between $V$ and $S$. Assign $w(s)=b(s)-r-1$ on $X$ and $w(s)=a(s)-t-\varepsilon$ on $Y$. Identify the zero-weight attachments with a new root $o$. At each positive attachment $s$, add two $o$--$s$ paths of length $w(s)$ with disjoint private interiors. Record the original endpoint of every attachment edge, and delete loops created by identification.

The resulting multigraph is connected and bridgeless. The depth of an ordinary vertex $v$ is exactly $b(v)-r-1$ on $X$, and $a(v)-t-\varepsilon$ on $Y$. The root eccentricities are at most $r-1+\varepsilon$ and $r$, respectively, and every added arm is geodesic. Strong orientations of these auxiliary graphs restore to a strong orientation of $G$ extending the fixed orientation of $H$.

\label{lem:independent-root-step}
Use the subgraph $A$ formed by the red--blue edges, their endpoint tree routes to the root, and every length-one arm pair. Construct every child graph by the first rule, and use $\mathcal C$ on red components and the reversal of $\mathcal C$ on blue components. For each red external return, including one from a red vertex whose tree path begins with a complete arm, choose a minimum available $\mathcal C$ child return before restoring its actual attachment; use the reversed rule for a blue external input. If the root has one distinct neighbour, call $\mathcal C$ on the graph with the parent root deleted, rooted at that neighbour and with no designated arms, and append the parent edge to a minimum child return.

Under the two requirements on retained edges and their original endpoints stated above, suppose $R<\rho$ and $\tau=\rho+R-D\in[0,R]$. Both ordinary parent directions have length at most $A_R-g$, where $g=(2\tau-9)_+$. The original pointwise bounds remain valid, and a selected positive return with actual arm $w$ satisfies $I_R(z)$ and $\min\{W_R(w),F_R-g\}$ on the same path. A selected positive return restored from a child through a length-one parent arm has length exactly the chosen child minimum plus one. Every external return from another red vertex and every external input to a blue ordinary vertex has length at most its corresponding child minimum plus $2R$. Reversing the entire orientation exchanges inputs and returns.

\label{lem:tail-estimates}
Let a selected auxiliary root path have length $m$, actual attachment $s$, and length $l$ after restoration. With $\varphi=a$ on $X$ and $\varphi=d+\rho-b$ on $Y$, the restored path satisfies
\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
&\text{source: }l-\varphi(s)&\text{target: }l+\varphi(s)\\
X&m-t+1-\varepsilon&m+t-1+2\varepsilon\\
Y&m-d-t+1-2\varepsilon&m+d+t+\varepsilon
\end{array}
\]
where each entry is an upper bound. The bounds also hold for an endpoint in $H$, with restored path length zero and numerical auxiliary length $m=w(s)$.

\label{lem:initial-estimate}
Suppose that the centre $p$, its farthest vertex $q$, and a shortest $p$--$q$ path $Q$ are chosen so that either the quantity $m$ corresponding to a noncritical crossing edge is at most $r+\varepsilon$, or $d$ is odd and some edge has covering radius at most $r$. In the edge-cover case, $G$ has a strong orientation of diameter at most $L+d-1$. Otherwise, form the initial central subgraph from the fixed shortest-path trees and the directed cycles through the reversed critical edge, and use the prescribed rooted orientations and selected paths in its external components. For odd $d$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:initial-odd}
\operatorname{diam}(\mathpalette{\overarrow@\arrowfill@\relbar\relbar\rightharpoonup}{G})\le L+\max\{2r+2t+6-2\gamma,\ 3r+2t+1-\gamma-\delta\}.
\end{equation}
For even $d$, every $b_0\le\delta$ gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:initial-even}
\begin{split}
\operatorname{diam}(\mathpalette{\overarrow@\arrowfill@\relbar\relbar\rightharpoonup}{G})\le L+\max\{&2t+3-2\gamma,\ 2r+2t+5-\gamma-b_0,\\
&4r+2t+2-2b_0,\ 3r+2t-\gamma-b_0-1\}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
In either parity, the resulting orientation has diameter at most $L+d+18$ whenever $\delta\ge r+t-8$.

The edge-cover alternative is odd; Lemma~\ref{lem:edge-cover} gives diameter at most $2r^2+4r+1=L+d-1$. For odd $d$, Lemma~\ref{lem:tail-estimates} gives common terms $r^2-\rho-\gamma$ and $r^2+\rho-\gamma$ for the source and target paths, respectively. The additions for positive $X$ returns, zero $X$ returns, and $Y$ returns are $2,d,-1$; those for $X$ inputs, positive $Y$ inputs, and zero $Y$ inputs are $0,2,d$. Since $2r^2+d=L$, Lemmas~\ref{lem:central-core} and~\ref{lem:shallow-attachments} give excesses over $L$ of at most $2h+4-2\gamma$, $2h+3-2\gamma$, $2h+2-2\gamma$, and $2h-2\gamma$, according as neither end, only the source, only the target, or both ends use the indicated zero attachments; the double-zero critical pair is excluded. For noncritical double-zero pairs this uses the actual path length in $H$ $2h+1$ and the sum of the restored outside path lengths $2A_r-2\gamma=L-1-2\gamma$. All these cases are bounded by the first term of \eqref{eq:initial-odd}, since $h\le r+t+1$. The restored outside path lengths for the critical pair sum to at most $L-1-\gamma-\delta$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:axis-choice} bounds the connecting path length in $H$ by $2h+r$. This proves the second term. If $\delta\ge r+t-8$, \eqref{eq:gamma-bounds} bounds these two excesses by $2r+16$ and $2r+14$, both at most $d+18$.

For even $d$, the initial case has $T=2h$ and $K=0$. We retain a variable form of the path calculation for reuse after rebuilding in Section~\ref{sec:rebuilding}. This calculation uses only the selected root-path bounds and the assumed additional terms for paths in $H$. Let $b_0$, possibly negative, be a lower bound for the savings in the three classes of selected $Y$ paths in the orientation being estimated. Thus $b_0\le\delta$ for the present orientation; after rebuilding, the same calculation uses $b_0=\delta'$ for the new orientation. Suppose the general addition from $H$ is at most $d+T+K$, reduced to $T+K$ for a zero $X$ source and to $T+1$ for a zero $Y$ target. Here $T,K\ge0$. Lemma~\ref{lem:tail-estimates} bounds $l-\varphi(s)$ by $F_{r-1}-\gamma-t+1$, $A_{r-1}-\gamma-t+1$, and $A_r-b_0-2r-t+1$ for positive $X$, zero $X$, and ordinary $Y$, respectively. The bounds on $l+\varphi(s)$ for ordinary $X$, positive $Y$, and zero $Y$ are $A_{r-1}-\gamma+t-1$, $F_r-b_0+2r+t$, and $A_r-b_0+2r+t$, respectively. Adding the term from $H$ and subtracting $L$ gives the following table. Rows specify the source side and return type, and columns specify the target side and input type.
\begingroup\small\setlength{\arraycolsep}{3pt}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:even-path-costs}
\begin{array}{c|ccc}
&X&Y\text{ positive}&Y\text{ zero}\\
X\text{ positive}&-2r+3+T+K-2\gamma&5+T+K-\gamma-b_0&5+T-\gamma-b_0\\
X\text{ zero}&-2r+T+K-2\gamma&2+T+K-\gamma-b_0&2r+T+2-\gamma-b_0\\
Y&T+K-b_0-\gamma&2r+2+T+K-2b_0&2r+2+T-2b_0
\end{array}
\end{equation}
\endgroup
For example, the top-left entry is $F_{r-1}+A_{r-1}+d+T+K-2\gamma-L$. In the first two columns, replacing a positive $X$ return by a zero return adds $2r-3$ to the restored path length and subtracts $2r$ from the term contributed by $H$, reducing the entry by three. For a positive $X$ or ordinary $Y$ return, replacing a positive $Y$ input by a zero input reduces the total by $K$. When both paths have zero attachments, the entry uses $T+1$ directly for the term contributed by $H$.

For the initial orientation, use $T=2h$ and $K=0$ in \eqref{eq:even-path-costs}. The double-zero entry improves to $T+r-1-\gamma-b_0$: the restored outside path lengths sum to at most $A_{r-1}+A_r-\gamma-b_0=L-\gamma-b_0$, and Lemmas~\ref{lem:shallow-attachments} and~\ref{lem:axis-choice} bound both relevant actual connecting path lengths in $H$ by $T+r-1$, as $r\ge2$. The resulting maximum is
\[
\begin{split}
\max\{-2r+3+2h-2\gamma,\ 5+2h-\gamma-b_0,\ 2r+2+2h-2b_0,\ 2h+r-1-\gamma-b_0\}.
\end{split}
\]
Now $2h\le2r+2t$ proves \eqref{eq:initial-even}. If $\delta\ge r+t-8$, take $b_0=r+t-8$, which is allowed even when negative. By \eqref{eq:gamma-bounds}, the four terms are at most $13$, $r+18$, $2r+18$, and $2r+12$, respectively, and each is at most $d+18$.

\label{lem:rebuilt-core}
Let $p$ be a centre, and let $U$ be a connected vertex set with attachment $\xi$. Suppose $a(\xi)=P=t+2$, $w\in U$ satisfies $a(w)=\rho$, and $\ell=\operatorname{dist}(\xi,w)=r-2+\varepsilon$. Assume that a $\xi$--$w$ geodesic has all its internal vertices in $U$, and that every vertex $v$ of $U$ or its attachment set has a $\xi$--$v$ path of length $a(v)-P\le\ell$. Suppose also that every path from $w$ to an attachment other than $\xi$, with internal vertices in $U$, has length at least $2\ell-K$, where $K\ge0$.

Choose the new shortest path $Q$ by concatenating a $p$--$\xi$ geodesic and the prescribed $\xi$--$w$ geodesic. Redefine $b(v)=\operatorname{dist}(w,v)$ and $X,Y$ using the same threshold $\theta$. There is a connected bridgeless subgraph $H$ containing all crossing edges, with a strong orientation obtained by directing each noncritical crossing-edge path forwards and its intervening segment of $Q$ backwards. Its retained $X$ paths lie in one shortest-path tree rooted at $p$. Its retained $Y$ paths lie in one tree directed towards $w$ before the edges of $Q$ are reversed. For a retained $Y$ vertex $v$, let $D(v)$ be its distance to $w$ in this tree, let $\eta(v)=D(v)-b(v)$, and let $Q_{\nu(v)}$ be the first vertex of $Q$ on its tree path. Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rebuilt-tree-bounds}
0\le\eta(v)\le K,\qquad 2\nu(v)+\eta(v)\le T,\qquad T=2P+K.
\end{equation}
